Z||000000||sparks||01-19-2001||10:29 AM||||Is there some trick to putting the VSS back in the transaxle?  I got a replacement from Ed Parks since I chewed my gear up on mine.  When I put the new one in still nothing.  Do you have to line it up with something or is the VSS Ed sent me dead and I just need to put the gear on my old VSS?<P>thanx<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||5
Z||000001||danny boy||01-20-2001||12:37 AM||||There is no trick.If it is not working then go ahead and try the new gear on the old sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||01-20-2001||04:06 AM||||Try pulling off the gear on the new one and placing it on your old sensor, since it sounds like your old sensor is ok except for the chewed up teeth.<P>No trick, at least for an 88 Isuzu transmission.  Just make sure the sensor is pushed down far enough where the sensors hold down piece sits flush with the transmission.  I had to pull it out a few times to make it fit right, before I tightened the hold down screw.   The hold down screw and hold down piece are not meant to force the sensor down into the transmission orifice.  It is simply use to keep the sensor in place.<BR> <BR>Hope this helps <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||7
Z||000003||sparks||01-21-2001||05:04 PM||||So umm, what turns the red gear on the end of the VSS?  A blue gear that's in the transaxle?<P>What holds that in place?  Is it possible I chewed it up too when I drove the car?  I'm finding blue pieces in the oil, so is that why the speedo didn't work with the new VSS?????????<P>What's involved in replacing that gear which I'm guessing needs replaced too?<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||5
